\newlabel{sec:results:ablation}{{6.4}{25}{Ablation}{subsection.6.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Contribution of the computed carbon input, with the shared parameters frozen in all three variants. (a) Signed RGR error per photoperiod. M0, which prescribes the carbon input, underestimates growth at every photoperiod by 41 to 45\%; M1 and M2 scatter on both sides of the targets within $\pm 15\%$, and their mean absolute errors of $9.12$ and $8.58\%$ are close enough that the Jarvis conductance layer is not what carries the improvement. (b) Peak assimilation reached by each variant, in the units of the carbon input. The prescribed form saturates near $11\,\mu $mol C$_6$ gFW$^{-1}$ h$^{-1}$ and varies by only $0.6$ across the four photoperiods, while both computed forms reach $17$; that ceiling is the mechanism behind the errors in panel (a).}}{26}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ablation}{{5}{26}{Contribution of the computed carbon input, with the shared parameters frozen in all three variants. (a) Signed RGR error per photoperiod. M0, which prescribes the carbon input, underestimates growth at every photoperiod by 41 to 45\%; M1 and M2 scatter on both sides of the targets within $\pm 15\%$, and their mean absolute errors of $9.12$ and $8.58\%$ are close enough that the Jarvis conductance layer is not what carries the improvement. (b) Peak assimilation reached by each variant, in the units of the carbon input. The prescribed form saturates near $11\,\mu $mol C$_6$ gFW$^{-1}$ h$^{-1}$ and varies by only $0.6$ across the four photoperiods, while both computed forms reach $17$; that ceiling is the mechanism behind the errors in panel (a)}{figure.5}{}}
