\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Global sensitivity of relative growth rate over the declared parameter ranges, averaged over the four photoperiods. (a) Total-order Sobol' index for each parameter, split into the first-order part and the interaction remainder $S_T - S_1$. Summed first-order indices are $0.201$, so parameters acting alone account for about a fifth of the variance. The summed total-order indices come to $4.68$: because a total-order index charges each interaction to every parameter involved in it, that sum exceeds unity and cannot be used as a denominator for a variance share. (b) Morris $\mu ^{*}$ against $\sigma $ for the same parameters. The two rankings agree at a Spearman correlation of $0.944$, and 15 of the 16 parameters lie above the $\sigma = \mu ^{*}$ line, which is the screening signature of effects that depend on where in the space they are evaluated.}}{27}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sens}{{6}{27}{Global sensitivity of relative growth rate over the declared parameter ranges, averaged over the four photoperiods. (a) Total-order Sobol' index for each parameter, split into the first-order part and the interaction remainder $S_T - S_1$. Summed first-order indices are $0.201$, so parameters acting alone account for about a fifth of the variance. The summed total-order indices come to $4.68$: because a total-order index charges each interaction to every parameter involved in it, that sum exceeds unity and cannot be used as a denominator for a variance share. (b) Morris $\mu ^{*}$ against $\sigma $ for the same parameters. The two rankings agree at a Spearman correlation of $0.944$, and 15 of the 16 parameters lie above the $\sigma = \mu ^{*}$ line, which is the screening signature of effects that depend on where in the space they are evaluated}{figure.6}{}}
